// Real explanations for the City & Guilds questions (replaces the short "Answer: ..." notes). Matched by question text.
(function(){const E={"Which Act is the main piece of health and safety legislation covering all workplaces in the UK?":"The Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974 is the main law covering safety in all UK workplaces. The other regulations are made under it.","Under the Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974, employees must…":"Employees must take reasonable care of themselves and others and co-operate with their employer. They must not interfere with safety measures.","The Electricity at Work Regulations 1989 apply to…":"The Electricity at Work Regulations 1989 apply to all electrical systems and equipment in all work activities, not just construction.","Which organisation enforces health and safety law on construction sites?":"The HSE enforces health and safety law on construction sites. Local authorities cover some other workplaces such as offices and shops.","A prohibition notice issued by an HSE inspector requires…":"A prohibition notice stops an activity immediately because there is a risk of serious injury. It takes effect at once.","An improvement notice requires the person served to…":"An improvement notice gives a stated time to put right a breach of the law. It does not stop work straight away.","The legal status of an Approved Code of Practice (ACOP) is that…":"An ACOP is not law in itself, but if you are prosecuted and did not follow it, you must show you met the law another way.","BS 7671 is…":"BS 7671 is a British Standard, not law in itself. Following it is a recognised way of meeting the law.","Which regulations cover the safe selection, maintenance and use of tools and machines at work?":"PUWER 1998 covers the safe selection, maintenance and use of tools and work equipment.","RIDDOR requires certain incidents to be reported to…":"RIDDOR requires certain injuries, diseases and dangerous occurrences to be reported to the HSE or the local authority.","COSHH regulations are concerned with…":"COSHH covers hazardous substances such as solvents, fumes and dust. It requires assessment and control of exposure.","Under the Work at Height Regulations 2005 the first step is to…":"The Work at Height Regulations say first avoid working at height where reasonably practicable. If you cannot, prevent falls, then reduce their effect.","Who must have a written health and safety policy?":"Employers with five or more employees must have a written health and safety policy.","Under CDM 2015, the construction phase plan is prepared by the…":"Under CDM 2015 the principal contractor prepares the construction phase plan. It sets out how health and safety will be managed on site.","Under the Health and Safety at Work etc. Act, an employee who deliberately misuses a safety device…":"Deliberately misusing or interfering with a safety device is an offence under the Act, because it puts others at risk.","The three elements of the fire triangle are…":"The fire triangle has heat, fuel and oxygen. Remove any one and the fire goes out.","Which extinguisher is safe to use on live electrical equipment?":"CO₂ extinguishers do not conduct electricity and leave no residue, so they are safe on live electrical equipment. Water and foam are not.","The label colour of a carbon dioxide extinguisher is…":"A CO₂ extinguisher has a black label.","The label colour of a foam extinguisher is…":"A foam extinguisher has a cream label.","A water extinguisher is suitable for…":"Water extinguishers suit Class A fires such as wood, paper and textiles. Never use them on live electrical or flammable-liquid fires.","Dry powder extinguishers are identified by a…":"Dry powder extinguishers have a blue label. They suit a wide range of fires but give poor visibility and are messy.","On discovering a fire, the first action is to…":"First raise the alarm, so everyone can get out and the fire service is called. Only then consider fighting a small fire if it is safe.","An electric current of around 30 mA or more through the chest may…":"A current of about 30 mA or more across the chest can cause ventricular fibrillation, where the heart quivers and stops pumping.","The first thing to do when a person is in contact with live equipment is to…":"Switch off the supply, or if you cannot, free the person with dry non-conducting material. Never touch them directly.","A blue circular sign with a white symbol indicates…":"A blue circular sign with a white symbol is a mandatory action, such as wear a hard hat.","A green rectangular sign shows…":"A green rectangular sign shows a safe condition, such as an exit or first-aid point.","A yellow triangle with a black border is a…":"A yellow triangle with a black border is a warning sign.","Used fluorescent tubes are classed as…":"Used fluorescent tubes contain mercury, so they are hazardous waste and must be disposed of through an authorised route.","The duty of care for waste means you must…":"Duty of care means you pass waste only to an authorised carrier and keep records, so it is handled correctly through to disposal.","Which item should NOT be in a workplace first-aid kit?":"Tablets and medicines should not be in a workplace first-aid kit. Only trained staff give them, and people may be allergic.","Burns from an electric shock should be cooled with…":"Cool burns with clean running water for at least 10 minutes to stop the damage spreading.","The upper exposure action value for noise (hearing protection mandatory) is…":"The upper exposure action value for noise is 85 dB(A), above which hearing protection is mandatory.","The first step in a five-step risk assessment is to…":"The first step is to identify the hazards. You cannot control what you have not found.","A hazard is…":"A hazard is anything with the potential to cause harm. Risk is the chance of that harm and how serious it would be.","Risk is best described as…":"Risk combines how likely harm is with how serious it would be. A rare event with severe harm can still be a high risk.","In the hierarchy of control, which is used last?":"In the hierarchy of control, PPE is used last. Remove or reduce the hazard first, and use PPE where a risk remains.","A method statement describes…":"A method statement describes the safe order in which the job will be done, step by step.","A permit to work is…":"A permit to work is formal written authority to carry out specific hazardous work, with the precautions listed.","The first step of safe isolation is to…":"The first step of safe isolation is to identify the circuit and its source of supply, so you isolate the right one.","After isolating a circuit you should…":"After isolating, secure the isolation with a personal lock and a warning notice, so nobody can switch it on again.","Why is the voltage indicator proved before and after testing?":"The voltage indicator is proved before and after testing to show it was working when you tested the circuit. A dead tester gives a false 'dead' reading.","To confirm a circuit is dead, voltage is tested between…":"Test between all conductors: line to neutral, line to earth and neutral to earth, to confirm the circuit is dead.","GS 38 recommends the maximum exposed length of voltage-indicator probe tips is…":"GS 38 limits the exposed probe tip to 4 mm, and 2 mm or less is better, so the probe cannot short two conductors.","A ladder should be set at an angle of about…":"A ladder should stand at about 75 degrees, the 1-in-4 rule. Too shallow and the base slips, too steep and it topples backward.","The 1-in-4 rule means…":"The 1-in-4 rule means the ladder base is 1 m out for every 4 m of height.","A ladder used to reach a landing should extend above it by at least…":"A ladder to a landing should extend at least 1 m above it so you have something to hold on to when stepping on and off.","Site hand tools are normally powered from…":"Site hand tools are normally powered from a 110 V centre-tapped earth supply, which limits shock voltage.","Why is the 110 V supply for site tools centre-tapped to earth?":"A centre-tapped 110 V supply gives only 55 V to earth from each line, which is far less likely to be fatal.","TILE in manual handling stands for…":"TILE stands for Task, Individual, Load, Environment. Use it to assess a lift.","Which PPE protects the eyes when drilling masonry?":"Safety glasses or goggles protect the eyes from dust and chips when drilling masonry.","A safety data sheet (SDS) gives information about…":"A safety data sheet describes a hazardous substance and how to store, use and dispose of it safely.","A mobile access tower should only be erected by…":"A mobile access tower must be erected only by trained, competent people, because errors cause falls and collapse.","Direct contact means touching…":"Direct contact means touching live conductive parts, for example a bare live terminal.","Indirect contact means touching…":"Indirect contact means touching a metal part that has become live because of a fault.","Before digging, you should…":"Before digging, get the service plans, use a cable avoidance tool and dig trial holes by hand to find buried services.","Suspected asbestos is found when you start drilling. You should…":"Suspected asbestos means stop work, leave the area and report it. Disturbing it can release deadly fibres.","A damaged extension lead should be…":"A damaged extension lead should be removed from use, labelled as defective and reported. Do not repair it with tape.","Solar PV panels remain live in daylight, so…":"Solar PV panels produce voltage whenever light falls on them. Isolating the DC circuit is not enough on its own, so treat the array as live.","A near miss should be…":"Report every near miss so the causes can be removed before someone is hurt.","Hand-arm vibration is a risk when using…":"Hand-arm vibration is a risk when using tools like a hammer drill for long periods.","Dust from chasing walls is controlled by…":"Control dust from chasing with extraction or wet cutting and a suitable respirator.","Before working near a large capacitor you should…":"Make sure a large capacitor is discharged safely before working near it, because it can hold a dangerous charge.","A lone worker should…":"A lone worker should tell someone where they are and check in at agreed times, so help is sent if something goes wrong.","If you find an unlabelled underground cable while digging, you should…":"Treat an unlabelled underground cable as live and stop work until it is identified.","What is the main danger of overhead power lines on a job?":"The main danger of overhead lines is touching them, or coming close, with ladders, poles or machinery.","A trailing cable across a walkway is a hazard because…":"A trailing cable across a walkway can cause trips and the cable may be damaged.","Which action helps keep an escape route safe?":"Keep escape routes clear of materials at all times so people can leave quickly in an emergency.","If an emergency occurs on site, the correct sequence is…":"In an emergency: raise the alarm, call the emergency services, give first aid if trained, and go to the assembly point.","Transposing V = I × R to make R the subject gives…":"V = I × R, so R = V ÷ I.","Transposing P = I² × R to make I the subject gives…":"P = I² × R, so I² = P ÷ R and I = √(P ÷ R).","What is 15% of 240?":"15% of 240 = 0.15 × 240 = 36.","Sharing 50 in the ratio 3:2 gives…":"3 + 2 = 5 parts, so 50 ÷ 5 = 10 per part. 3 parts = 30 and 2 parts = 20.","4,700 in standard form is…":"4,700 = 4.7 × 1,000 = 4.7 × 10³.","Using BODMAS, 3 + 4 × 2 equals…":"BODMAS says multiply before adding: 4 × 2 = 8, then 3 + 8 = 11.","The cross-sectional area of a round conductor of 10 mm diameter is about…":"Area = π × r² = 3.142 × 5² = 78.5 mm².","The solution to 2x + 5 = 17 is…":"2x + 5 = 17, so 2x = 12 and x = 6.","An impedance triangle has resistance 30 Ω and reactance 40 Ω. The impedance is…":"Z = √(R² + X²) = √(30² + 40²) = 50 Ω.","If the length of a cable doubles but everything else stays the same, its resistance…":"Resistance is proportional to length, so doubling the length doubles the resistance.","The SI unit of power is the…":"The SI unit of power is the watt.","The SI unit of energy is the…":"The SI unit of energy is the joule.","The SI unit of force is the…":"The SI unit of force is the newton.","The SI unit of electric charge is the…":"The SI unit of electric charge is the coulomb.","The SI unit of capacitance is the…":"The SI unit of capacitance is the farad.","The SI unit of inductance is the…":"The SI unit of inductance is the henry.","The SI unit of magnetic flux density is the…":"The SI unit of magnetic flux density is the tesla.","The prefix \"kilo\" means…":"The prefix kilo means 1,000.","The prefix \"milli\" means…":"The prefix milli means one thousandth, 0.001.","2.5 kΩ is equal to…":"2.5 kΩ = 2.5 × 1,000 = 2,500 Ω.","0.5 A is equal to…":"0.5 A × 1,000 = 500 mA.","One kilowatt-hour is equal to…":"1 kWh = 1,000 W × 3,600 s = 3.6 million joules, or 3.6 MJ.","50 µF (microfarads) is equal to…":"50 µF = 50 × 10⁻⁶ F = 0.000 05 F.","1 mm² is equal to…":"1 mm = 10⁻³ m, so 1 mm² = (10⁻³)² = 10⁻⁶ m².","To convert degrees Celsius to kelvin…":"To convert °C to kelvin, add 273. 0°C is 273 K.","The weight of a 20 kg mass (g = 9.81 m per second²) is…":"Weight = mass × g = 20 × 9.81 = 196.2 N.","Work done is calculated as…":"Work done = force × distance moved in the direction of the force.","Lifting 30 kg through 4 m requires about…":"Work = m × g × h = 30 × 9.81 × 4 = 1,177 J.","Power is the…":"Power is the rate of doing work, in joules per second (watts).","Work of 1,177 J done in 10 s represents a power of…":"Power = work ÷ time = 1,177 ÷ 10 = 117.7 W.","A motor takes 2,000 W and delivers 1,700 W. Its efficiency is…":"Efficiency = output ÷ input × 100 = 1,700 ÷ 2,000 × 100 = 85%.","A force of 150 N acts at the end of a 0.4 m spanner. The torque is…":"Torque = force × distance = 150 × 0.4 = 60 N m.","A motor produces 20 N m at 1,500 rpm. Its power output is about…":"Power = torque × angular speed. 1,500 rpm = 157.1 rad/s, so P = 20 × 157.1 = 3.14 kW.","A 400 N load is balanced on a lever with a load arm of 0.3 m and an effort arm of 1.2 m. The effort needed is…":"Effort × effort arm = load × load arm. Effort = 400 × 0.3 ÷ 1.2 = 100 N.","A 20-tooth driver gear at 1,200 rpm drives a 60-tooth gear. The driven gear speed is…":"Driven speed = driver speed × driver teeth ÷ driven teeth = 1,200 × 20 ÷ 60 = 400 rpm.","The potential energy of a 10 kg mass lifted 5 m is about…":"Potential energy = m × g × h = 10 × 9.81 × 5 = 490 J.","The kinetic energy of a 10 kg mass moving at 4 m/s is…":"Kinetic energy = ½ m v² = 0.5 × 10 × 16 = 80 J.","Mechanical advantage is…":"Mechanical advantage is load ÷ effort.","A pulley system has a velocity ratio of 4. A load of 500 N is raised by an effort of 150 N. The efficiency is about…":"Mechanical advantage = 500 ÷ 150 = 3.33. Efficiency = MA ÷ VR = 3.33 ÷ 4 = 83%.","The energy needed to heat 2 kg of water by 80 degrees (c = 4,180 J per kg per degree) is about…":"Energy = m × c × ΔT = 2 × 4,180 × 80 = 668,800 J, about 669 kJ.","A 3 kW heater that is 100% efficient heats the above water in about…":"Time = energy ÷ power = 668,800 ÷ 3,000 = 223 s.","Pressure is calculated as…":"Pressure is force divided by the area it acts on.","A 230 V supply across a 46 Ω resistor gives a current of…":"I = V ÷ R = 230 ÷ 46 = 5 A.","A circuit passes 0.5 A at 12 V. The resistance is…":"R = V ÷ I = 12 ÷ 0.5 = 24 Ω.","2 A flowing through 15 Ω produces a potential difference of…":"V = I × R = 2 × 15 = 30 V.","Three resistors of 10, 20 and 30 Ω in series have a total resistance of…":"In series, resistances add: 10 + 20 + 30 = 60 Ω.","12 Ω and 4 Ω in parallel have a total resistance of…":"12 Ω in parallel with 4 Ω = (12 × 4) ÷ (12 + 4) = 3 Ω.","Three 30 Ω resistors in parallel give…":"Three equal resistors in parallel: 30 ÷ 3 = 10 Ω.","The total resistance of resistors in parallel is always…":"Parallel paths give extra routes for current, so the total is always less than the smallest resistor.","In a series circuit the…":"In a series circuit there is only one path, so the same current flows through every component.","In a parallel circuit the…":"In a parallel circuit every branch is across the same two points, so the voltage is the same across each.","A 120 V supply feeds 10, 20 and 30 Ω in series. The current is…":"I = V ÷ R = 120 ÷ 60 = 2 A.","A 6 Ω resistor in series with 12 Ω and 4 Ω in parallel has a total resistance of…":"12 Ω ∥ 4 Ω = 3 Ω. Total = 6 + 3 = 9 Ω.","12 V is applied across 1 kΩ and 3 kΩ in series. The pd across the 3 kΩ resistor is…":"Total resistance is 4 kΩ, so I = 12 ÷ 4,000 = 3 mA. Across 3 kΩ: 3 mA × 3,000 = 9 V.","Two identical 230 V lamps are wired in series across 230 V. Each lamp has…":"Two equal lamps in series share the supply, so each gets 115 V and is dim.","Charge moved by 5 A flowing for 20 seconds is…":"Q = I × t = 5 × 20 = 100 C.","The unit of resistivity is…":"Resistivity is measured in ohm metres (Ω m).","The resistance of 100 m of 1.5 mm² copper (resistivity 1.72 × 10⁻⁸ Ω metre) is about…":"R = ρL ÷ A = 1.72 × 10⁻⁸ × 100 ÷ 1.5 × 10⁻⁶ = 1.15 Ω.","Doubling the cross-sectional area of a conductor will…":"Doubling the cross-sectional area halves the resistance, because R is inversely proportional to area.","Copper has…":"Copper has a positive temperature coefficient. Its resistance rises as the temperature rises.","A 0.5 Ω copper conductor at 20 degrees is heated to 70 degrees (coefficient 0.004 per degree). Its resistance is…":"R = 0.5 × (1 + 0.004 × 50) = 0.5 × 1.2 = 0.6 Ω.","Which material has a negative temperature coefficient?":"Carbon has a negative temperature coefficient: its resistance falls as it gets hotter.","The power taken by a 230 V appliance drawing 13 A is about…":"P = V × I = 230 × 13 = 2,990 W, about 3 kW.","5 A flowing through 8 Ω dissipates…":"P = I² × R = 25 × 8 = 200 W.","A 3 kW, 230 V heater draws a current of about…":"I = P ÷ V = 3,000 ÷ 230 = 13 A.","A 3 kW heater used for 4 hours consumes…":"Energy = 3 kW × 4 h = 12 kWh.","At 25 pence per kWh, 12 kWh costs…":"Cost = 12 kWh × 25p = 300p = £3.00.","The heating effect of a current is proportional to…":"The heating effect is I² × R × t. Doubling the current makes four times the heat.","A 25 m run of 2.5 mm² copper twin cable carries 16 A. The voltage drop is about…":"Voltage drop = I × R. Using the resistivity of copper, R is about 0.344 Ω for the 2 × 25 m run, so 16 × 0.344 = 5.5 V.","Four 1.5 V cells in series give…":"Cells in series add their voltages: 4 × 1.5 = 6 V.","Cells connected in parallel give…":"Cells in parallel keep the voltage the same and give greater current capacity.","A battery of 12 V and internal resistance 0.1 Ω delivers 20 A. Its terminal voltage is…":"Terminal voltage = EMF − I × r = 12 − (20 × 0.1) = 10 V.","A nominal lead-acid cell has a voltage of about…":"A nominal lead-acid cell is about 2 V, so a 12 V battery has six cells in series.","An ammeter is connected…":"An ammeter is connected in series so the circuit current flows through it.","A voltmeter is connected…":"A voltmeter is connected in parallel across the component, because it measures the potential difference between two points.","An ideal voltmeter has…":"An ideal voltmeter has very high resistance, so it takes almost no current and does not change the circuit.","A shunt resistor used to extend the range of an ammeter is connected…":"A shunt resistor in parallel with the meter takes most of the current, so the meter can read larger currents.","Ohm's law states that at constant temperature, the current in a conductor is…":"Ohm's law: at constant temperature the current is proportional to the potential difference across the conductor.","A primary cell is one that…":"A primary cell cannot be recharged. A secondary cell can.","The SI unit of magnetic flux is the…":"The SI unit of magnetic flux is the weber (Wb).","The SI unit of flux density is the…":"The SI unit of flux density is the tesla (T), which is one weber per square metre.","Fleming's left-hand rule is used to find the direction of…":"Fleming's left-hand rule gives the direction of force on a current-carrying conductor, which is motor action.","Fleming's right-hand rule is used for…":"Fleming's right-hand rule gives the direction of the induced EMF, which is generator action.","The force on a 0.2 m conductor carrying 10 A at right angles to a field of 0.5 T is…":"F = B × I × L = 0.5 × 10 × 0.2 = 1 N.","A conductor 0.5 m long moves at 10 m/s at right angles to a field of 0.8 T. The induced EMF is…":"EMF = B × L × v = 0.8 × 0.5 × 10 = 4 V.","Lenz's law states that the induced EMF…":"Lenz's law: the induced EMF acts to oppose the change that produces it.","A transformer has 1,000 primary turns and 100 secondary turns on 230 V. The secondary voltage is…":"Vs = 230 × 100 ÷ 1,000 = 23 V.","Laminating a transformer core reduces…":"Laminating the core breaks up the paths for eddy currents, so there is less eddy current loss and heating.","Soft iron is used for transformer cores because…":"Soft iron magnetises and demagnetises easily, so little energy is lost in each cycle.","A 4-pole alternator running at 1,500 rpm produces a frequency of…":"f = pn ÷ 120 = 4 × 1,500 ÷ 120 = 50 Hz.","A sine wave with a peak of 325 V has an RMS value of about…":"RMS = peak ÷ √2 = 325 ÷ 1.414 = 230 V.","In a purely inductive AC circuit, the current…":"In a purely inductive circuit the current lags the voltage by 90°.","In a purely capacitive AC circuit, the current…":"In a purely capacitive circuit the current leads the voltage by 90°.","The inductive reactance of a 0.2 H coil at 50 Hz is about…":"X_L = 2πfL = 2 × 3.142 × 50 × 0.2 = 62.8 Ω.","Windings in a three-phase generator are displaced by…":"The three windings of a three-phase generator are placed 120 degrees apart.","In a star connection the line voltage is…":"In a star connection the line voltage is √3 times the phase voltage.","A transformer will only work on…":"A transformer needs a changing magnetic flux, so it works only with alternating current.","The direction of the magnetic field around a straight current-carrying conductor is found with…":"The right-hand grip rule (corkscrew rule) gives the direction of the field around a straight current-carrying conductor.","Electroplating is an example of which effect of current?":"Electroplating uses the chemical effect of an electric current.","A resistor coloured red, red, brown, gold has a value of…":"Red = 2, red = 2, brown = ×10, gold = ±5%. So 220 Ω ±5%.","A resistor coloured yellow, violet, red, gold has a value of…":"Yellow = 4, violet = 7, red = ×100, gold = ±5%. So 4,700 Ω = 4.7 kΩ ±5%.","A resistor coloured brown, black, orange, silver has a value of…":"Brown = 1, black = 0, orange = ×1,000, silver = ±10%. So 10 kΩ ±10%.","A diode conducts when it is…":"A diode conducts when it is forward-biased, with the anode positive of the cathode.","The forward voltage drop of a silicon diode is about…":"A silicon diode drops about 0.7 V when conducting.","A 12 V supply drives a 2 V, 20 mA LED. The series resistor is…":"R = (12 − 2) V ÷ 0.02 A = 500 Ω.","Capacitors connected in parallel have a total capacitance that…":"Capacitors in parallel add: the total capacitance is the sum of the individual values.","The time constant of a 10 kΩ resistor and a 100 µF capacitor is…":"The time constant is R × C = 10,000 × 0.0001 = 1 second.","A full-wave bridge rectifier uses…":"A full-wave bridge rectifier uses four diodes.","A thyristor is turned on by…":"A thyristor is turned on by a pulse at its gate and stays on until the current falls.","A component whose resistance falls when light falls on it is a…":"A light-dependent resistor falls in resistance as light falls on it.","An electrolytic capacitor must be…":"An electrolytic capacitor is polarised, so it must be connected with the correct polarity or it can fail.","A Zener diode is mainly used for…":"A Zener diode is mainly used for voltage reference and regulation, working in reverse breakdown.","The energy stored in a 1,000 µF capacitor charged to 25 V is…":"Energy = ½ C V² = 0.5 × 0.001 × 25² = 0.3125 J.","A transistor can be used as a switch because…":"A transistor can be used as a switch because a small base current controls a much larger collector current.","Which regulations place duties on all electrical work to ensure safety and require competent people?":"The Electricity at Work Regulations 1989 place duties on all electrical work and require competent people.","BS 7671 is published by…":"BS 7671 is published by the IET with BSI.","Part 6 of BS 7671 deals with…":"Part 6 of BS 7671 covers inspection and testing.","Part 7 of BS 7671 deals with…":"Part 7 covers special installations or locations, such as bathrooms and swimming pools.","Part 4 of BS 7671 deals with…":"Part 4 covers protection for safety: shock, thermal effects and overcurrent.","Approved Document P of the Building Regulations covers…":"Approved Document P of the Building Regulations covers electrical safety in dwellings.","An Electrical Installation Certificate is issued for…":"An EIC is issued for new installations and alterations that include new circuits.","A Minor Electrical Installation Works Certificate is used for…":"A Minor Works Certificate is used for additions or alterations that do not include a new circuit.","An Electrical Installation Condition Report (EICR) is issued after…":"An EICR is issued after periodic inspection of an existing installation.","A competent person scheme allows registered installers to…":"A competent person scheme lets registered installers self-certify notifiable work.","The declared UK supply voltage tolerance is…":"The declared supply voltage is 230 V with a tolerance of +10% and −6%.","The Electricity Safety, Quality and Continuity Regulations apply mainly to…":"The Electricity Safety, Quality and Continuity Regulations apply mainly to network operators and suppliers.","A drawing showing the positions of luminaires, switches and sockets on a floor plan is a…":"A layout drawing shows the positions of luminaires, switches and sockets on a floor plan.","A drawing showing how a circuit works in symbol form is a…":"A circuit diagram shows how a circuit works in symbol form.","A drawing showing connections between terminals as they will be wired is a…":"A wiring diagram shows the connections between terminals as they will be wired.","Symbols for electrical drawings follow…":"Electrical drawing symbols follow BS EN 60617.","On a 1:50 scale drawing, a 40 mm line represents…":"40 mm × 50 = 2,000 mm = 2 m.","On a 1:100 drawing, a wall 6 m long measures…":"6 m = 6,000 mm. 6,000 ÷ 100 = 60 mm.","A specification describes…":"A specification describes the standard of materials and workmanship required.","A bill of quantities lists…":"A bill of quantities lists the items and quantities of work and materials.","As-fitted drawings show…":"As-fitted drawings show the installation as completed.","In BS 7671 terminology, Ib means…":"Ib is the design current of the circuit.","In BS 7671 terminology, In means…":"In is the nominal current rating of the protective device.","In BS 7671 terminology, Iz means…":"Iz is the current-carrying capacity of the cable in the installed conditions.","Zs is…":"Zs is the total earth fault loop impedance.","Ze is…":"Ze is the external earth fault loop impedance of the supply.","R1 + R2 is…":"R1 + R2 is the resistance of the line conductor plus the circuit protective conductor.","Ca is the correction factor for…":"Ca is the correction factor for ambient temperature.","The \"IP\" in an IP rating stands for…":"IP stands for ingress protection. The two digits give protection against solids and water.","The symbol of a square inside a square on an appliance indicates…":"A square inside a square marks Class II (double-insulated) equipment, which has no earth connection.","Prospective fault current is…":"Prospective fault current is the highest current that would flow in a fault of negligible impedance at that point.","In single-phase installations the harmonised colour for the line conductor is…":"Under harmonised colours, the line conductor in single-phase is brown.","The harmonised colour for the neutral conductor is…":"The harmonised neutral colour is blue.","In a three-phase system the harmonised colours of L1, L2 and L3 are…":"In three-phase, L1, L2 and L3 are brown, black and grey.","The bare CPC in flat twin and earth cable must be sleeved…":"The bare CPC is sleeved green-and-yellow at terminations so it is clearly identified as the earth.","The maximum continuous conductor operating temperature of PVC cable is…":"Standard PVC cable is limited to a conductor temperature of 70°C.","XLPE insulation allows a maximum conductor temperature of…":"XLPE insulation allows a conductor temperature of 90°C, so it carries more current.","Which cable has steel wire armour that can act as a protective conductor?":"SWA cable has steel wire armour that can act as the protective conductor.","Which cable is used for fire alarm circuits that must keep working in a fire?":"MI or FP200-type cable is fire-resistant, so alarms keep working in a fire.","LSZH stands for…":"LSZH stands for low smoke zero halogen, which gives little toxic smoke in a fire.","Flexible cord must not be used for…":"Flexible cord is not for fixed wiring because it is not made to be fixed and protected like fixed cable.","Cut ends of conduit are deburred to…":"Deburring cut conduit ends stops sharp edges damaging cable insulation.","The maximum space factor for trunking is…":"The maximum space factor for trunking is 45%, leaving room for heat to escape and for cables to be drawn in.","The clip spacing for non-armoured cable up to 9 mm diameter run horizontally is…":"Clips for non-armoured cable up to 9 mm diameter run horizontally are spaced at 250 mm.","Cable of 9 to 15 mm overall diameter run vertically has a clip spacing of…":"Cable of 9 to 15 mm diameter run vertically is clipped at 400 mm spacing.","PVC cables should not be installed when the temperature is below…":"PVC becomes stiff and brittle in the cold, so it should not be installed below 0°C.","A cable concealed in a wall at less than 50 mm depth should run…":"Cables concealed in a wall less than 50 mm deep must run in a safe zone or be protected, for example by RCD or earthed metal.","Fire barriers are fitted in trunking…":"Fire barriers in trunking stop fire and smoke spreading through floors and fire compartment walls.","Cable tray is mainly used to…":"Cable tray supports heavy armoured and multicore cables.","Armoured cable buried underground is typically installed at a depth of at least…":"Buried armoured cable is installed at a depth of at least 450 mm in normal ground.","Warning tape above a buried cable is used to…":"Warning tape tells anyone digging that a cable is buried below, so they stop before reaching it.","Switches in a lighting circuit must break the…":"Switches in a lighting circuit must break the line conductor, so the lamp holder is dead when switched off.","The loop-in method of wiring lighting means that…":"In a loop-in system the supply cable is looped from one ceiling rose to the next, so there are no joints hidden.","A typical protective device for a domestic lighting circuit is…":"Domestic lighting circuits are commonly protected by a 6 A device.","In a two-way switching circuit the live feed goes to…":"In a two-way circuit the live feed goes to the common (COM) terminal of the first switch.","The strappers in a two-way circuit connect…":"The strappers connect L1 to L1 and L2 to L2 between the two switches.","A cable passing through a metal box should be fitted with…":"A cable entering a metal box needs a grommet or bush to protect its sheath from sharp edges.","Joints and connections must be accessible for inspection unless they are…":"Joints must stay accessible for inspection unless they are maintenance-free types.","Cables in thermal insulation must be…":"Cables in thermal insulation lose heat poorly, so their current rating must be reduced (derated).","MI cable ends are sealed to…":"MI cable ends are sealed to keep moisture out of the magnesium oxide insulation.","If a blue core is used as a switch live in a lighting circuit it must be…":"A blue core used as a switch live must be identified with brown sleeving or tape at its terminals.","The typical minimum bending radius for non-armoured PVC cable of up to 10 mm diameter is…":"For non-armoured PVC cable up to 10 mm diameter, the minimum bending radius is about 4 times the overall diameter.","Steel conduit systems must be…":"Steel conduit must be electrically continuous and earthed so it cannot become live.","A fused connection unit (FCU) for a 3 kW heater would use a fuse of…":"A 3 kW heater on 230 V draws about 13 A, so a 13 A fuse is used in the FCU.","A typical ring final circuit is protected by a…":"A typical ring final circuit is protected by a 32 A device.","A ring final circuit wired in 2.5 mm² flat cable can serve a floor area of about…":"A ring in 2.5 mm² cable can serve a floor area of about 100 m².","A 20 A radial socket circuit in 2.5 mm² cable serves a floor area of up to about…":"A 20 A radial in 2.5 mm² cable serves a floor area of up to about 50 m².","The number of unfused spurs from a ring circuit must not exceed…":"The number of unfused spurs must not exceed the number of sockets and fixed appliances connected directly on the ring.","A Type B MCB trips instantly at…":"A Type B MCB trips instantly at 3 to 5 times its rated current.","A Type C MCB trips instantly at…":"A Type C MCB trips instantly at 5 to 10 times its rated current.","Which MCB type would you choose for a circuit with high inrush current such as a transformer?":"Type D tolerates the very high inrush current of transformers, so it is chosen for them.","The relationship that must be met for overload protection is…":"For overload protection the rule is Ib ≤ In ≤ Iz.","The breaking capacity of a protective device must be…":"The breaking capacity must be at least the prospective fault current at its location.","An RCD detects…":"An RCD detects an imbalance between the line and neutral currents, which means current is leaking to earth.","Additional protection by RCD of 30 mA is required for…":"Socket outlets rated up to 32 A for use by ordinary persons need additional protection by a 30 mA RCD.","A 30 mA RCD should disconnect within… at its rated residual current.":"A 30 mA RCD should disconnect within 300 ms at its rated residual current.","A 30 mA RCD should disconnect within… at five times its rated residual current.":"At five times its rated current a 30 mA RCD should trip within 40 ms.","What is the maximum earth fault loop impedance for a Type B 32 A MCB (0.4 s)?":"The maximum Zs for a Type B 32 A MCB (0.4 s) is about 1.37 Ω. A higher loop impedance means too little fault current to trip it quickly.","Measured Zs values are usually compared with about… of the tabulated maximum.":"Measured Zs is compared with about 80% of the tabulated maximum, to allow for the conductors warming up in use.","The maximum disconnection time for a final circuit of 32 A or less on a TN system is…":"The maximum disconnection time for final circuits up to 32 A on a TN system is 0.4 s.","A 7.2 kW shower on 230 V has a design current of about…":"Ib = 7,200 ÷ 230 = 31.3 A.","Voltage drop limits for a 230 V installation are normally…":"The usual voltage drop limits are 3% for lighting and 5% for other uses.","A 5% voltage drop on a 230 V supply is…":"5% of 230 V = 0.05 × 230 = 11.5 V.","The voltage drop for 18 mV/A/m, 20 A and 18 m is…":"Voltage drop = 18 × 20 × 18 ÷ 1,000 = 6.48 V.","A cable must carry It ≥ In ÷ (Ca × Cg × Ci). If In = 20 A, Ca = 0.94 and Cg = 0.80, the minimum tabulated rating is about…":"It ≥ In ÷ (Ca × Cg) = 20 ÷ (0.94 × 0.80) = 26.6 A.","Cable surrounded by thermal insulation has a correction factor (Ci) of about…":"A cable surrounded by thermal insulation has Ci of about 0.5, because heat cannot escape.","The correction factor for a BS 3036 semi-enclosed fuse is…":"The correction factor for a BS 3036 semi-enclosed fuse is 0.725.","Fault protection for Class II equipment is provided by…":"Class II equipment is protected against faults by double or reinforced insulation.","Basic protection means protection against…":"Basic protection is protection against contact with live parts.","Consumer units in domestic premises should have…":"Domestic consumer units should have a non-combustible (metal) enclosure so a fault cannot start a fire.","The purpose of diversity is…":"Diversity avoids over-sizing cables and devices, because not every load is used at once.","A surge protective device (SPD) protects against…":"A surge protective device protects against transient overvoltages from lightning or switching.","A cooker circuit is typically protected by…":"A cooker circuit is typically 32 A in 6 mm² cable.","In a consumer unit the main switch is used for…":"The main switch of a consumer unit isolates the whole installation.","A device that provides isolation but no overcurrent protection is…":"An isolator provides isolation but no overcurrent protection.","In a TN-S system the earth return path for the installation is…":"In a TN-S system the earth return is a separate conductor or the cable sheath back to the source.","TN-C-S is also known as…":"TN-C-S is also known as protective multiple earthing (PME).","In a TT system the earth connection is provided by…":"In a TT system the earth connection is the consumer's own earth electrode.","Which system must have RCD protection for fault protection?":"A TT system must have RCD protection for fault protection, because the earth path is high resistance.","Main protective bonding connects…":"Main protective bonding connects incoming metal services and structural steel to the main earthing terminal.","The minimum size of copper main bonding conductor for a TN-S system is…":"The minimum copper main bonding size for TN-S is 6 mm².","A typical main bonding conductor for a PME (TN-C-S) domestic supply with a neutral up to 35 mm² is…":"A typical main bonding conductor for a PME supply with a neutral up to 35 mm² is 10 mm².","Supplementary bonding with mechanical protection is a minimum of…":"Supplementary bonding with mechanical protection is at least 2.5 mm².","Supplementary bonding without mechanical protection is a minimum of…":"Supplementary bonding without mechanical protection is at least 4 mm².","For a TT system the electrode resistance condition is…":"For a TT system the electrode resistance must satisfy Ra × IΔn ≤ 50 V.","The maximum disconnection time for TN final circuits up to 32 A is…":"The maximum disconnection time for TN final circuits up to 32 A is 0.4 s.","The maximum disconnection time for TT final circuits up to 32 A is…":"The maximum disconnection time for TT final circuits up to 32 A is 0.2 s.","The maximum disconnection time for distribution circuits on a TN system is…":"The maximum disconnection time for distribution circuits on a TN system is 5 s.","Total earth fault loop impedance is…":"Zs = Ze + (R1 + R2).","A metal incoming water pipe is an example of…":"A metal incoming water pipe is an extraneous-conductive-part, so it is bonded to earth.","A metal motor frame is an example of…":"A metal motor frame is an exposed-conductive-part that could become live in a fault.","The main earthing terminal is where…":"The main earthing terminal is where the earthing conductor, CPCs and main bonding conductors meet.","A BS 951 clamp is used to…":"A BS 951 clamp connects a bonding conductor to a metal pipe safely.","A concern with PME supplies is that…":"With PME, if the combined neutral-earth (PEN) conductor breaks, exposed metalwork can be raised to a dangerous voltage.","Electricity is transmitted at high voltage to…":"Higher transmission voltage means less current for the same power, so I²R losses fall.","The UK transmission grid operates at…":"The UK transmission grid operates at 400 kV and 275 kV.","Local substations normally step 11 kV down to…":"Local substations normally step 11 kV down to 400/230 V.","The voltage between a line and neutral in a UK supply is…":"Line to neutral on a UK single-phase supply is 230 V.","The voltage between two lines of the UK three-phase supply is…":"Between two lines of the UK three-phase supply the voltage is 400 V, which is 230 × √3.","The cut-out is…":"The cut-out is the network operator's fuse and service termination at the origin of the installation.","Extra-low voltage (ELV) is defined as not exceeding…":"Extra-low voltage is not more than 50 V AC or 120 V ripple-free DC.","Low voltage (LV) AC is above 50 V and up to…":"Low voltage AC is above 50 V and up to 1,000 V.","The frequency of the UK mains supply is…":"The UK mains frequency is 50 Hz.","A solar photovoltaic panel produces…":"A photovoltaic panel produces direct current (DC).","The component that converts DC from PV panels to AC is the…":"The inverter converts the DC from PV panels into AC for the installation and the grid.","Loss-of-mains protection in a PV inverter is intended to…":"Loss-of-mains protection shuts the inverter down when the grid fails, so the installation does not feed power back to a network that workers think is dead.","A heat pump with a COP of 3.5 delivers…":"A COP of 3.5 means 3.5 kW of heat for each 1 kW of electrical input.","Small generators connected to the network up to 16 A per phase are covered by…":"Small generators up to 16 A per phase connect under G98, a connect-and-notify process.","Solar thermal collectors produce…":"Solar thermal collectors produce heat for hot water.","Which BS 7671 section covers solar PV installations?":"BS 7671 Section 712 covers solar photovoltaic installations.","Which BS 7671 section covers EV charging installations?":"BS 7671 Section 722 covers electric vehicle charging installations.","The Smart Export Guarantee (SEG) is…":"The Smart Export Guarantee requires licensed suppliers to pay for renewable electricity exported to the grid.","Which of these is a micro-renewable that produces heat only?":"A biomass boiler is a micro-renewable that produces heat only.","Before drilling into a wall you should…":"Check for hidden cables and pipes before drilling, using a detector or the known safe zones.","When cutting conduit, the end should be…":"Conduit should be cut square and deburred inside and outside, so edges do not cut the cables.","A set or bend in conduit should…":"A conduit set or bend should be smooth, without kinks or flattening, so cables can be drawn in.","An offset in conduit is made with…":"An offset uses two equal bends to take the conduit round an obstruction.","A saddle bend in conduit is used to…":"A saddle bend takes conduit over an obstruction such as a pipe.","Cutting oil is used when…":"Cutting oil is used when threading steel conduit with a stock and die, to cool the tool and get a clean thread.","The correct tool to form a bend in PVC conduit is…":"A bending spring supports the PVC conduit, so it bends without flattening. Use it with care and gentle heat.","The steel conduit system must be…":"Steel conduit must be mechanically strong, electrically continuous and earthed.","A red wall plug is used for a hole of…":"A red wall plug is used for a 6 mm hole.","Trunking lids are normally fitted…":"Fit trunking lids after the cables are installed and tested, so they can be checked.","Trunking dividers are fitted to…":"Dividers keep circuits of different bands separated inside the trunking.","On a steel trunking run, earth continuity across joints is provided by…":"Links or bonding connections across joints give earth continuity in steel trunking.","When setting out, a datum is…":"A datum is a fixed starting line or point from which all measurements are taken.","The most useful tool to check that a run is level is a…":"A spirit level shows whether a run is level.","The first thing to do before starting a practical assignment is to…":"Before starting a practical, read the brief and drawing and do the risk assessment, so you know what is required and how to do it safely.","A cable passing through a metal box edge should be protected by…":"A grommet or bush protects a cable passing through a metal box edge.","Cable clips on flat PVC cable should be…":"Clips on flat PVC cable should be evenly spaced, tight and the correct size for a neat, secure run.","Cable tray is cut with…":"Cable tray is cut with a hacksaw or tray cutter, and the cut edges are deburred.","At a cable tray, cables should be…":"Cables on tray should be fixed securely at the correct spacing with ties, clips or cleats.","Cables entering a trunking from outside should have…":"Cables entering trunking from outside need grommets or a bush to protect the insulation.","The first dead test normally carried out on a circuit is…":"The first dead test is normally continuity of protective conductors (R1 + R2).","Insulation resistance on a 230 V circuit is tested with…":"Insulation resistance on a 230 V circuit is tested with 500 V DC.","The minimum acceptable insulation resistance for an LV circuit is…":"The minimum acceptable insulation resistance for an LV circuit is 1 MΩ.","Before an insulation resistance test the lamps should be…":"Before an insulation resistance test remove lamps and disconnect sensitive equipment, because the test voltage can damage it.","The bare CPC of flat twin and earth must be…":"The bare CPC is sleeved green-and-yellow at terminations.","When making a termination, the correct amount of insulation is stripped so that…":"Strip just enough insulation so no bare copper shows outside the terminal and no insulation is trapped inside.","In an Edison screw lampholder, the line conductor connects to…":"In an Edison screw lampholder the line connects to the centre contact, so the exposed screw thread is not live.","In a socket outlet the line conductor connects to the terminal marked…":"The line conductor of a socket outlet connects to the terminal marked L.","A cord grip on a flexible cord is used to…":"A cord grip removes strain from the terminals when the cord is pulled.","An SWA gland provides earth continuity by…":"An SWA gland clamps the armour wires between the cone and the gland body, which gives earth continuity.","A circuit is tested for polarity to check that…":"The polarity test checks that switches and protective devices are in the line conductor and connections are correct.","In the ring final circuit test, r2 for 2.5/1.5 mm² cable is about… times r1.":"For 2.5/1.5 cable the cpc is thinner, so its resistance is higher, roughly 1.6 to 1.7 times r1.","During testing of the circuit you should keep your own lock on the isolator because…":"Keep your own lock on the isolator so nobody can energise the circuit while you are testing.","The last step of a practical assignment should be…":"The last step is to tidy up and segregate waste after the final checks.","Who is responsible for the overall design of the building and its appearance?":"The architect designs the building and its appearance.","Who pays for and owns the project?":"The client pays for and owns the project.","Which member of the construction team prepares the bill of quantities and manages costs?":"The quantity surveyor prepares the bill of quantities and manages costs.","The main contractor is responsible for…":"The main contractor is responsible for delivering the whole project and managing subcontractors.","An electrical contractor working for a main contractor is a…":"An electrical contractor working for a main contractor is a subcontractor.","Who designs the mechanical and electrical services of a large building?":"The building services (M&E) consulting engineer designs the mechanical and electrical services.","Who runs the site day to day, including the programme and site safety?":"The site manager runs the site day to day, including the programme and site safety.","The distribution network operator (DNO) is responsible for…":"The distribution network operator provides and connects the electrical supply.","Good teamwork on site includes…":"Good teamwork means co-ordinating access and timing with the other trades.","First fix work is done…":"First fix work is done before plastering and finishes.","Discrimination and harassment on site are…":"Discrimination and harassment on site are unacceptable and against the law.","The key (legend) on a drawing explains…":"The key (legend) explains the symbols and abbreviations used on a drawing.","A specification gives…":"A specification gives the standard and quality of materials and work.","A programme shows…":"A programme shows the order and timing of work.","A delivery note should be checked for…":"Check a delivery note for the quantity and condition of goods on delivery.","A variation order is…":"A variation order is a written instruction that changes the scope, time or cost of the work.","A timesheet is used to record…":"A timesheet records hours worked.","Manufacturers' installation instructions provide…":"Manufacturers' installation instructions give details of how to fit, wire and use the product.","Before using a drawing you should check…":"Before using a drawing check that it is the latest revision.","Information about the safe way to carry out a task is found in the…":"The risk assessment and method statement set out the safe way to carry out a task.","If two drawings disagree you should…":"If two drawings disagree, ask your supervisor or the designer before continuing. Do not guess.","Which is an example of verbal communication?":"A toolbox talk is a spoken briefing, so it is verbal communication.","Which is an example of written communication?":"An email or job sheet is written communication.","Active listening involves…":"Active listening is paying attention, asking questions and confirming that you understood.","A barrier to communication is…":"Noise and jargon are barriers to communication.","When taking a telephone message you should note…":"Note the date, time, caller's name, number and message when taking a phone message.","If you are given a verbal instruction that changes the cost, you should…":"If a verbal instruction changes the cost, confirm it in writing so there is a record.","When dealing with a customer complaint you should…":"With a complaint, listen calmly, record it and pass it to your supervisor.","Body language is a form of…":"Body language is non-verbal communication.","A hazard found on site should be…":"Report a hazard found on site immediately.","With customers you should use…":"With customers use plain language and avoid unexplained jargon.","An email to a colleague should have…":"An email should have a clear subject line and a brief, polite message.","Good communication on site helps to…":"Good communication on site helps to prevent mistakes and accidents.","Which person checks on behalf of the client that the work is built to the required quality?":"The clerk of works (or client inspector) checks on behalf of the client that the work is built to the required quality.","Under CDM 2015 the principal contractor is mainly responsible for…":"Under CDM 2015 the principal contractor plans, manages and co-ordinates the construction phase and its health and safety.","Building control officers visit a site to…":"Building control officers visit a site to check that the work complies with the Building Regulations.","An apprentice on site should…":"An apprentice works under supervision and asks when unsure.","A purchase order is…":"A purchase order is a document sent to a supplier to order goods.","The revision letter on a drawing tells you…":"The revision letter tells you which version of the drawing it is.","A daywork sheet is used to record…":"A daywork sheet records extra work done on a time-and-materials basis.","A site plan shows…":"A site plan shows the position of the building and services on the site.","Which document gives the order and timing of tasks and is often a bar chart?":"The programme gives the order and timing of tasks and is often a bar chart.","Where would you find how a particular consumer unit must be wired?":"The manufacturer's installation instructions tell you how a consumer unit must be wired.","The abbreviation O&M manual stands for…":"O&M stands for operation and maintenance manual.","Which is an example of graphical communication?":"A sketch or drawing is graphical communication.","Repeating an instruction back to the person who gave it shows…":"Repeating an instruction back shows you have understood it.","A toolbox talk is…":"A toolbox talk is a short briefing on a safety topic given on site.","At handover the customer should be given…":"At handover the customer should receive certificates, test results and an explanation of how to use the installation.","In the energy hierarchy for buildings, the first step is to…":"The energy hierarchy starts with reducing the energy demand, because the cheapest energy is the energy you do not use.","A renewable energy source is one that…":"A renewable energy source is replenished naturally.","Which Building Regulations Part covers electrical safety in dwellings?":"Part P of the Building Regulations covers electrical safety in dwellings.","Which Building Regulations Part covers conservation of fuel and power?":"Part L covers conservation of fuel and power.","The Microgeneration Certification Scheme (MCS) certifies…":"MCS certifies installers and products used for microgeneration.","The Smart Export Guarantee requires licensed suppliers to…":"The Smart Export Guarantee requires licensed suppliers to pay for low-carbon electricity exported to the grid.","Generators up to 16 A per phase connect to the network under…":"Generators up to 16 A per phase connect under G98.","Which section of BS 7671 covers solar PV systems?":"Section 712 of BS 7671 covers solar PV systems.","Who may handle the refrigerant in a heat pump?":"Only a person holding a recognised F-gas qualification may handle the refrigerant in a heat pump.","An Energy Performance Certificate rates a building on a scale from…":"An EPC rates a building from A (most efficient) to G (least efficient).","Waste PV modules and batteries are covered by…":"Waste PV modules and batteries are covered by the WEEE and battery waste regulations.","Standard test conditions (STC) for a PV module are an irradiance of…":"STC is an irradiance of 1,000 W/m² at a cell temperature of 25°C.","The unit kWp refers to…":"kWp is the peak (STC) power rating of the array.","A 4 kWp system producing about 900 kWh per kWp per year generates about…":"Energy = 4 kWp × 900 kWh/kWp = 3,600 kWh per year.","The best orientation for PV in the UK is…":"In the UK the best PV orientation is south-facing at a tilt of about 30 to 40 degrees.","The open-circuit voltage of a PV string is highest…":"The open-circuit voltage is highest on a cold, bright day, because modules produce more voltage when cold.","Ten modules, each with a cold-weather Voc of 45.6 V, are connected in series. The string Voc is…":"String Voc = 10 × 45.6 V = 456 V.","Modules connected in series…":"Series modules add their voltages.","Strings connected in parallel…":"Parallel strings add their currents at the same voltage.","The DC isolator on a PV system must be…":"The DC isolator must be rated for DC at the array's voltage and current, because DC arcs are harder to break.","Loss-of-mains protection in a PV inverter…":"Loss-of-mains protection stops the inverter feeding the network when the grid fails.","A 400 W module with an area of 1.95 m² has an efficiency of about…":"Efficiency = 400 W ÷ (1.95 m² × 1,000 W/m²) = 0.205, or 20.5%.","Typical commissioning measurements on a PV string include…":"Commissioning a PV string includes checking polarity, open-circuit voltage and short-circuit current.","A reason for using microinverters or optimisers is to…":"Microinverters and optimisers limit the effect of shading on individual modules.","Which cable is normally used on the DC side of a PV array?":"The DC side of a PV array uses UV-resistant double-insulated solar cable.","A PV array on a roof in daylight…":"A PV array cannot be switched off in daylight, so it is live whenever light falls on it.","The power available in the wind is proportional to…":"The power in the wind is proportional to the cube of the wind speed.","The theoretical maximum fraction of wind power a turbine can capture (Betz limit) is…":"The Betz limit is 59.3%, the theoretical maximum fraction of wind power a turbine can capture.","If the wind speed doubles, the power in the wind becomes…":"Because power depends on the cube of the speed, doubling the wind speed gives 2³ = 8 times the power.","A 5 m diameter rotor in an 8 m/s wind with Cp = 0.35 produces about…":"P = ½ × ρ × A × v³ × Cp = 0.5 × 1.225 × 19.6 × 512 × 0.35 ≈ 2.2 kW.","A good site for a small wind turbine has…":"A good site has an average wind speed above about 5 m/s and little turbulence.","Micro-hydro power is calculated from…":"Micro-hydro power depends on flow rate, head and efficiency.","A hydro scheme with 0.05 m³/s flow, 20 m head and 60% efficiency produces about…":"P = ρ × g × Q × H × η = 1,000 × 9.81 × 0.05 × 20 × 0.6 = 5.9 kW.","Which turbine suits high head and low flow?":"A Pelton wheel suits high head and low flow.","An abstraction licence is needed for…":"An abstraction licence is needed to take water from a river for a hydro scheme.","A heat pump’s refrigerant absorbs heat from the source in the…":"The refrigerant absorbs heat from the source in the evaporator.","The component of a heat pump that uses the main electrical input is the…":"The compressor uses the main electrical input of a heat pump.","The coefficient of performance (COP) of a heat pump is…":"COP is the heat output divided by the electrical input.","A heat pump with a COP of 3.5 and an electrical input of 2 kW delivers…":"Heat output = COP × input = 3.5 × 2 = 7 kW.","Heat pumps usually perform best with…":"Heat pumps perform best with low flow temperatures, such as underfloor heating.","Compared with air, the ground temperature is…":"The ground is more stable in temperature through the year than the air.","A differential temperature controller in a solar thermal system switches the pump on when…":"The differential controller switches the pump on when the collector is a few degrees hotter than the cylinder.","To control Legionella, stored hot water should regularly reach…":"To control Legionella, stored hot water should regularly reach 60°C.","The ideal (Carnot) COP for a 5 degree source and a 35 degree flow is about…":"Carnot COP = T_hot ÷ (T_hot − T_cold) = 308 ÷ 30 = 10.3 (in kelvin).","Handling the refrigerant in a heat pump needs…":"Handling the refrigerant in a heat pump needs an F-gas qualification.","A rainwater harvesting system is normally used for…":"Rainwater harvesting is for non-drinking uses such as WC flushing.","The mains top-up to a rainwater tank has an air gap to…":"An air gap in the mains top-up prevents stored water flowing back into the mains.","A 100 m² roof with 0.7 m annual rainfall, runoff 0.8 and filter efficiency 0.9 yields about…":"Yield = 100 × 0.7 × 0.8 × 0.9 = 50.4 m³ per year.","Greywater recycling normally uses water from…":"Greywater recycling uses water from baths, showers and hand basins.","Stored greywater should be used…":"Stored greywater should be used within a short time because bacteria multiply.","Pipework carrying non-drinking water should be…":"Non-drinking water pipework should be clearly labelled and kept separate from drinking water.","A 5 kWh battery with 90% usable depth of discharge gives…":"Usable energy = 5 kWh × 0.9 = 4.5 kWh.","A battery management system (BMS) protects the battery against…":"A BMS protects a battery against overcharge, overdischarge and over-temperature.","An AC-coupled battery connects…":"An AC-coupled battery connects through its own inverter to the AC side of the installation.","Which section of BS 7671 covers EV charging?":"BS 7671 Section 722 covers EV charging.","A 32 A single-phase EV charge point provides about…":"P = 230 × 32 = 7.4 kW for a single-phase 32 A charge point.","A 32 A three-phase charge point at 400 V gives about…":"P = √3 × 400 × 32 = 22 kW for a three-phase 32 A charge point.","On a PME supply an EV charge point needs…":"On a PME supply an EV charge point needs protection against loss of the PEN conductor, by an open-PEN device or an earth electrode.","CHP stands for…":"CHP stands for combined heat and power.","A micro-CHP unit is usually…":"A micro-CHP unit is usually heat-led, with electricity as a by-product.","Biomass fuel includes…":"Biomass fuel includes wood pellets, logs and chips.","Battery installations should be located…":"Battery installations should be ventilated, away from escape routes, and follow the manufacturer's guidance.","Why is a Type A RCD with 6 mA DC detection used for EV charging?":"A fault in the vehicle can put smooth DC leakage on the circuit, which would blind a basic RCD. A Type A RCD with 6 mA DC detection can still operate.","The angular frequency ω of a 50 Hz supply is…":"ω = 2πf = 2 × 3.142 × 50 = 314.2 rad/s.","The RMS value of a sine wave with a peak of 325 V is about…":"RMS = peak ÷ √2 = 325 ÷ 1.414 = 230 V.","The average value over a half cycle of a sine wave is…":"The average value over a half cycle of a sine wave is 0.637 × the peak.","The form factor of a sine wave is…":"The form factor of a sine wave is RMS ÷ average = 1.11.","The peak (crest) factor of a sine wave is…":"The crest factor of a sine wave is peak ÷ RMS = 1.414.","For e = 325 sin(ωt) at 50 Hz, the instantaneous voltage at t = 2 ms is about…":"e = 325 × sin(2π × 50 × 0.002) = 325 × sin(0.628 rad) = 325 × 0.588 = 191 V.","If i = 5 sin(ωt − 30°) and v = 100 sin(ωt), the current…":"A current angle of −30° means the current reaches its peak after the voltage, so it lags by 30 degrees.","Two voltages 100 V and 60 V are 90 degrees apart. Their phasor sum is about…":"At 90 degrees the phasors add as the hypotenuse: √(100² + 60²) = 116.6 V.","An oscilloscope shows 4 divisions peak-to-peak at 50 V per division. The RMS value is about…":"4 divisions × 50 V = 200 V peak-to-peak, so the peak is 100 V and RMS = 100 ÷ 1.414 = 70.7 V.","A trace has a period of 5 divisions at 2 ms per division. The frequency is…":"The period is 5 × 2 ms = 10 ms, so the frequency is 1 ÷ 0.01 = 100 Hz.","In a purely inductive circuit the current…":"In a purely inductive circuit the current lags the voltage by 90°.","In a purely capacitive circuit the current…":"In a purely capacitive circuit the current leads the voltage by 90°.","The inductive reactance of 0.1 H at 50 Hz is about…":"X_L = 2πfL = 2π × 50 × 0.1 = 31.4 Ω.","The capacitive reactance of 20 µF at 50 Hz is about…":"X_C = 1 ÷ (2πfC) = 1 ÷ (2π × 50 × 20 × 10⁻⁶) = 159 Ω.","A series circuit has R = 30 Ω and X(L) = 31.42 Ω. The impedance is about…":"Z = √(R² + X²) = √(30² + 31.42²) = 43.4 Ω.","That circuit on 230 V draws a current of about…":"I = V ÷ Z = 230 ÷ 43.4 = 5.3 A.","The power factor of the same circuit is about…":"Power factor = R ÷ Z = 30 ÷ 43.4 = 0.69.","A series circuit has R = 40 Ω, X(L) = 100 Ω and X(C) = 70 Ω. The impedance is…":"Net reactance = 100 − 70 = 30 Ω. Z = √(40² + 30²) = 50 Ω.","A 40 Ω resistor in parallel with an inductive reactance of 30 Ω on 120 V draws a total current of…":"In parallel the branch currents are at 90°: I_R = 3 A, I_L = 4 A, so the total is √(3² + 4²) = 5 A.","The resonant frequency of a series L-C circuit is…":"At resonance X_L = X_C, which gives f = 1 ÷ (2π√(LC)).","The resonant frequency of 50 mH and 20 µF is about…":"f = 1 ÷ (2π × √(0.05 × 20 × 10⁻⁶)) = 1 ÷ (2π × 0.001) = 159 Hz.","At series resonance the impedance is…":"At series resonance the reactances cancel, so the impedance is a minimum, equal to R.","At series resonance the current is…":"At series resonance the impedance is smallest, so the current is a maximum and in phase with the voltage.","The quality factor of a series resonant circuit with X(L) = 50 Ω and R = 10 Ω is…":"Q = X_L ÷ R = 50 ÷ 10 = 5.","At parallel resonance the circuit impedance is…":"At parallel resonance the circuit impedance is a maximum, because very little current is drawn from the supply.","A 100 Ω resistor in series with 20 µF at 230 V and 50 Hz has a power factor of about…":"X_C = 159 Ω, Z = √(100² + 159²) = 188 Ω, so pf = 100 ÷ 188 = 0.53, leading because it is capacitive.","True power in an AC circuit is calculated as…":"True power = V × I × cos φ.","Apparent power is measured in…":"Apparent power is measured in volt-amperes (VA).","Reactive power Q is calculated as…":"Reactive power Q = V × I × sin φ, measured in var.","A load of 230 V and 5.29 A at a power factor of 0.69 takes real power of about…":"P = 230 × 5.29 × 0.69 = 840 W, about 841 W.","The reactive power needed to correct 841 W from pf 0.69 to 0.95 is about…":"Reactive power to remove = P × (tan φ₁ − tan φ₂) = 841 × (1.049 − 0.329) ≈ 604 var.","The capacitor for that correction on 230 V, 50 Hz is about…":"C = Q ÷ (2π f V²) = 604 ÷ (314.2 × 230²) = 36 µF.","A low lagging power factor results in…":"A low lagging power factor means higher current for the same real power, which increases losses and cable heating.","Third harmonic currents in a three-phase four-wire system…":"Third harmonic currents are in phase in all three lines, so they add up in the neutral instead of cancelling.","Non-linear loads such as LED drivers and switch-mode supplies produce…":"Non-linear loads such as LED drivers and switch-mode supplies draw current in pulses, which produces harmonic currents.","In a delta connection the line current is…":"In a delta connection the line current is √3 times the phase current.","The power in a balanced three-phase load is…":"The power in a balanced three-phase load is √3 × V_L × I_L × cos φ.","A three-phase load draws 20 A at 400 V and power factor 0.8. The real power is about…":"P = √3 × 400 × 20 × 0.8 = 11.1 kW.","Three 20 Ω resistors on 400 V in star dissipate about…":"Each resistor sees 400 ÷ √3 = 231 V. P = 3 × 231² ÷ 20 = 8 kW.","The same three resistors in delta dissipate…":"In delta each resistor sees the full 400 V, which is √3 times higher, so the power is three times the star power.","The neutral current of line currents 10 A, 8 A and 6 A (resistive) is about…":"For resistive loads the neutral current is √(10² + 8² + 6² − 10×8 − 8×6 − 10×6) = √12 = 3.5 A.","To reverse a three-phase motor you…":"Swapping any two of the three lines reverses the rotating field and so the motor.","In the two-wattmeter method, total power is…":"In the two-wattmeter method the total power is W1 + W2.","Readings of 5 kW and 2 kW in the two-wattmeter method give a total of…":"Total = 5 + 2 = 7 kW.","The transformer EMF equation is…":"The transformer EMF equation is E = 4.44 × f × N × Φm.","A transformer has 500 turns, 50 Hz and peak flux 2.5 mWb. The EMF is about…":"E = 4.44 × 50 × 500 × 0.0025 = 277.5 V.","Transformer iron losses are measured by…":"The open-circuit test measures iron (core) losses, because at no load the copper loss is negligible.","Transformer copper losses are measured by…":"The short-circuit test measures copper losses, because the flux and iron loss are very small at low voltage.","A transformer is most efficient when…":"A transformer is most efficient when copper loss equals iron loss.","A 50 kVA transformer at full load and pf 0.8 has iron loss 400 W and copper loss 700 W. The efficiency is about…":"Output = 50 kVA × 0.8 = 40 kW. Losses = 0.4 + 0.7 = 1.1 kW. Efficiency = 40 ÷ 41.1 = 97.3%.","A secondary falling from 240 V to 230 V on load has a regulation of…":"Regulation = (240 − 230) ÷ 240 × 100 = 4.2%.","A 500 kVA, 400 V transformer with 4% impedance has a maximum fault current of about…":"Full load = 500,000 ÷ (√3 × 400) = 722 A. Fault current = 722 ÷ 0.04 = 18 kA.","A distribution transformer that provides a neutral is usually connected…":"A distribution transformer that provides a neutral is delta-star: delta primary and star secondary.","The synchronous speed of a 4-pole motor on 50 Hz is…":"Ns = 120 × 50 ÷ 4 = 1,500 rpm.","A 4-pole motor running at 1,450 rpm on 50 Hz has a slip of…":"Slip = (1,500 − 1,450) ÷ 1,500 = 3.33%.","An 11 kW motor at 88% efficiency, 400 V and pf 0.85 draws a current of about…":"Input = 11 ÷ 0.88 = 12.5 kW. I = 12,500 ÷ (√3 × 400 × 0.85) = 21 A.","A DC motor on 230 V with 40 A armature current and 0.3 Ω armature resistance has a back-EMF of…":"Eb = V − Ia × Ra = 230 − (40 × 0.3) = 218 V.","A 4-pole alternator at 1,500 rpm produces a frequency of…":"f = p × n ÷ 120 = 4 × 1,500 ÷ 120 = 50 Hz.","Capacitance is defined as…":"Capacitance is charge divided by voltage: C = Q ÷ V.","Doubling the plate area of a capacitor will…":"Capacitance is proportional to plate area, so doubling the area doubles the capacitance.","Halving the plate separation will…":"Capacitance is inversely proportional to plate separation, so halving the gap doubles it.","Two equal capacitors connected in series have a total capacitance of…":"Capacitors in series add like resistors in parallel. Two equal capacitors in series give half of one.","A 100 µF capacitor at 50 V holds a charge of…":"Q = C × V = 100 × 10⁻⁶ × 50 = 5 × 10⁻³ C = 5 mC.","The energy stored in 100 µF at 50 V is…":"Energy = ½ C V² = 0.5 × 100 × 10⁻⁶ × 50² = 0.125 J.","The time constant of a capacitor charging circuit is…":"The time constant of a charging capacitor is τ = R × C.","After one time constant a charging capacitor has reached about…":"After one time constant a charging capacitor has reached about 63% of the final voltage.","A capacitor is regarded as fully charged after about…":"A capacitor is regarded as fully charged after about five time constants.","R = 50 kΩ and C = 100 µF have a time constant of…":"τ = 50,000 × 100 × 10⁻⁶ = 5 s.","With a 12 V supply, the capacitor voltage after one time constant is about…":"After one time constant V = 12 × 0.632 = 7.6 V.","The induced EMF in a 0.5 H inductor when the current changes at 20 A/s is…":"Induced EMF = L × di/dt = 0.5 × 20 = 10 V.","The energy stored in a 0.5 H inductor carrying 4 A is…":"Energy = ½ L I² = 0.5 × 0.5 × 4² = 4 J.","The time constant of a 2 H inductor with 10 Ω resistance is…":"Time constant = L ÷ R = 2 ÷ 10 = 0.2 s.","Suppression diodes are fitted across relay coils to…":"A diode across a relay coil absorbs the back-EMF when the coil is switched off, protecting the switching device.","Magnetomotive force is calculated as…":"Magnetomotive force = N × I, measured in ampere-turns.","A ring with 500 turns, 2 A and a mean length of 0.4 m has a field strength of…":"H = N × I ÷ l = 500 × 2 ÷ 0.4 = 2,500 A/m.","Reluctance is the magnetic circuit equivalent of…":"Reluctance is the magnetic circuit equivalent of resistance.","Magnetic saturation means that…":"At saturation, more field strength adds very little extra flux.","The area of a hysteresis loop represents…":"The area of the hysteresis loop represents the energy lost per cycle as heat.","A Wheatstone bridge with R1 = 100 Ω, R2 = 200 Ω and R3 = 150 Ω balances when R4 equals…":"A bridge balances when R1 ÷ R2 = R3 ÷ R4, so R4 = R2 × R3 ÷ R1 = 200 × 150 ÷ 100 = 300 Ω.","Kirchhoff’s current law states that…":"Kirchhoff's current law: the sum of currents entering a node equals the sum leaving it.","A moving-iron meter can measure…":"A moving-iron meter responds to current in either direction, so it can measure both AC and DC.","A shunt used to extend the range of an ammeter is…":"A shunt is a low-value resistor in parallel with the meter, so most current bypasses it and a small meter can read a larger current.","An open-circuit fault is…":"An open-circuit fault is a break in a conductor or connection.","A short circuit between line and neutral typically causes…":"A line-neutral short circuit draws a very high current, which operates the protective device.","A high-resistance connection is dangerous mainly because it…":"A high-resistance connection heats up and may cause fire or arcing, even at normal current.","The first step in the fault-finding process is to…":"The first step in fault finding is to gather information from the client and records, so you know what is happening and when.","After finding the fault and repairing it, you should…":"After repairing a fault, test and verify the circuit before restoring it to service.","In the half-split method you…":"In the half-split method you test at the middle of the circuit and repeat on the half that holds the fault, which finds it quickly.","Fault finding on a circuit normally starts with…":"Fault finding starts with safe isolation and dead tests where possible, so it is safe to work.","A fault that causes the RCD to trip is most likely to involve…":"A fault that trips an RCD usually involves current leaking to earth.","A borrowed neutral means that…":"A borrowed neutral is a neutral shared between two circuits.","Stored energy hazards during fault finding include…":"Stored-energy hazards during fault finding include capacitors, batteries and PV arrays.","A low-resistance ohmmeter used for continuity tests should have a test current of at least…":"A continuity ohmmeter should supply at least 200 mA.","An insulation resistance test on a 230 V circuit uses…":"Insulation resistance on a 230 V circuit uses 500 V DC.","Voltage indicators for proving dead should comply with…":"Voltage indicators for proving dead should comply with GS 38.","A leakage clamp meter is used to…":"A leakage clamp meter measures small leakage currents without disconnecting conductors.","A thermal imaging camera is useful for finding…":"A thermal imaging camera finds hot joints and overloaded conductors.","A time domain reflectometer is used to…":"A time domain reflectometer sends a pulse down the cable and uses the echo to locate the distance to a fault.","A TDR echo returns after 2 µs on a cable with a signal speed of 2 × 10⁸ m/s. The fault is at…":"Distance = speed × time ÷ 2 = 2 × 10⁸ × 2 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 2 = 200 m. Divide by 2 because the pulse goes there and back.","A phase rotation meter is used to…":"A phase rotation meter confirms the phase sequence, so a motor turns the right way.","A multifunction tester should be…":"A multifunction tester must be calibrated regularly and its leads checked before use.","A loop impedance tester on a low-current (no-trip) setting is used to…":"A no-trip setting measures Zs without tripping the RCD.","The test leads of a continuity tester are nulled to…":"Nulling removes the resistance of the leads from the reading.","A clamp meter measures current by…":"A clamp meter senses the magnetic field around a conductor, so no break in the circuit is needed.","Which fault-finding method starts at the supply and works along the circuit until the condition changes?":"Input to output starts at the supply and works along the circuit until the condition changes.","The safe isolation procedure is used before dead fault-finding tests to…":"Safe isolation makes sure the circuit cannot be energised while it is being tested.","A 60 m ring using 2.5 mm² cable has an end-to-end line resistance of about…":"R = 7.41 mΩ/m × 60 m = 0.44 Ω.","Cross-connecting line and neutral at the consumer unit, the reading at each socket should be about…":"With line and neutral cross-connected, each socket should read about (r1 + rn) ÷ 4.","If the line conductor of a ring final circuit reads open-circuit end to end, the circuit…":"An open line conductor in a ring may still work as two radial legs, but there is a break to find and repair.","One socket reading higher than the others in the ring cross-connection test suggests…":"A higher reading at one socket suggests a spur or a loose or high-resistance connection there.","A lamp is off but the lampholder remains live. The likely fault is…":"If a lamp is off but the lampholder remains live, the switch is in the neutral instead of the line.","A lamp that works from only one of two two-way switches suggests…":"A lamp that works from only one of two two-way switches suggests an open-circuit strapper or crossed L1 and L2.","Flickering LED lighting on a dimmer is often caused by…":"Flickering LED lighting on a dimmer is often caused by an incompatible dimmer or a failing driver.","A low insulation resistance reading is traced by…":"Find low insulation resistance by testing line-earth, neutral-earth and line-neutral separately and by sectioning the circuit.","An insulation resistance reading of 0.3 MΩ on a 230 V circuit is…":"0.3 MΩ is below the 1 MΩ minimum. It is a fail and the fault must be found.","Low insulation readings can be misleading if…":"Low readings can be misleading if surge protection or electronic equipment is left connected during the test.","A TDR is most useful for…":"A TDR is most useful for finding a fault on a long or buried cable.","Before testing SWA cable insulation you should…":"Before testing SWA cable insulation, isolate and prove dead, then test between cores and between cores and armour.","The CPC end-to-end resistance of a 2.5/1.5 mm² ring is about… times r1.":"The cpc end-to-end resistance of a 2.5/1.5 mm² ring is about 1.6 to 1.7 times r1.","A short circuit between line and neutral will normally…":"A line-neutral short circuit draws a high current that should operate the MCB.","A burnt terminal at a socket is most likely caused by…":"A burnt terminal at a socket is most likely caused by a loose connection with high resistance.","A reading of 0.22 Ω at every socket on a ring cross-connected L-N indicates…":"Each socket reads (r1 + rn) ÷ 4. A constant value like 0.22 Ω at every socket shows a continuous ring with no spurs.","An RCD that trips immediately and will not reset suggests…":"An RCD that trips immediately and will not reset suggests a hard earth fault.","An RCD that trips only when several appliances run together may indicate…":"An RCD that trips only when several appliances run may be seeing their combined leakage exceed the RCD rating.","To avoid nuisance tripping on a circuit with high combined leakage, you can…":"To avoid nuisance tripping on a circuit with high combined leakage, divide the loads over separate RCBOs.","A shared neutral between two RCD-protected circuits can cause…":"A shared neutral between two RCD-protected circuits can make the RCD trip when load is applied.","A high earth fault loop impedance may be caused by…":"A high Zs may be caused by a loose or damaged cpc or a high-resistance joint.","On a PME supply, an open-circuit PEN conductor in the network may…":"On a PME supply, an open-circuit PEN conductor in the network can raise exposed metalwork to a dangerous voltage.","If you suspect an open PEN you should…":"If you suspect an open PEN, stop work, make safe and inform the network operator.","Nuisance tripping of a type B MCB on a motor start is solved by…":"A Type B MCB trips at a low multiple of its rating, so a motor start can trip it. A Type C or D suits the starting current.","The earth electrode resistance in a TT system may rise because of…":"The earth electrode resistance in a TT system may rise because of corrosion or dry soil.","An overload relay should be set to…":"An overload relay should be set to the motor nameplate full-load current.","A three-phase motor that hums but will not start often has…":"A three-phase motor that hums but will not start often has a missing phase (single-phasing) or a mechanical jam.","To reverse the direction of a three-phase motor…":"Swap any two of the three supply lines to reverse a three-phase motor.","A motor starts but stops when the start button is released. The likely fault is…":"If a motor stops when the start button is released, the hold-in (auxiliary) contact is open or wrongly wired.","Winding insulation resistance of a motor tested at 500 V should be at least…":"Winding insulation resistance tested at 500 V should be at least 1 MΩ.","A contactor that chatters (rapidly clicks) may have…":"A chattering contactor may have low control voltage or a loose control connection.","After rectifying a fault you should…":"After rectifying a fault, re-test the circuit and record the findings.","If a repair alters an installation, the certificate used is…":"If a repair alters an installation, use a Minor Electrical Installation Works Certificate.","Code C1 on an inspection report means…":"Code C1 means danger is present and immediate remedial action is required.","Finding the root cause after a repair is important because…":"Finding the root cause after a repair matters because it prevents the same fault from recurring.","An RCD that trips only in wet weather most probably has…":"An RCD that trips only in wet weather most probably has leakage to earth through moisture in outdoor equipment or cables.","Initial verification applies to…":"Initial verification applies to new installations and to alterations or additions.","Periodic inspection and testing is carried out to…":"Periodic inspection and testing check whether an existing installation is still safe and satisfactory.","The results of inspection are recorded on…":"The results of inspection are recorded on the Schedule of Inspections.","Inspection is carried out…":"Inspection is carried out before testing, with the installation isolated.","Single-pole switching and protective devices must be connected in…":"Single-pole switching and protective devices must be connected in the line conductor only.","During the initial verification, the recommended first test is…":"The recommended first test is continuity of protective conductors.","The correct sequence of tests places insulation resistance…":"Insulation resistance comes after continuity of protective conductors and ring continuity.","Live tests should not be carried out until…":"Do not carry out live tests until the dead tests are satisfactory.","If any test shows a fault, you should…":"If any test shows a fault, correct it and repeat the earlier tests that may be affected.","Before insulation testing, electronic equipment should be…":"Disconnect electronic equipment before insulation testing to avoid damage and false readings.","The person carrying out verification must be…":"The person carrying out verification must be competent.","Which is NOT normally checked during visual inspection?":"The colour of the paint on the wall is not checked in visual inspection.","A low-resistance ohmmeter for continuity should have a test current of at least…":"A continuity ohmmeter should have a test current of at least 200 mA.","In the R1 + R2 test, the line conductor and the CPC are linked…":"In the R1 + R2 test the line conductor and the cpc are linked at the distribution board, and the reading is taken at the far end.","For 2.5/1.5 mm² flat cable, R1 + R2 is about…":"For 2.5/1.5 mm² flat cable, R1 + R2 is about 19.5 mΩ per metre.","A 20 m run of 2.5/1.5 mm² cable should give an R1 + R2 of about…":"20 m × 19.5 mΩ/m = 390 mΩ = 0.39 Ω.","If the measured R1 + R2 is much higher than expected, this suggests…":"A measured R1 + R2 much higher than expected suggests a poor connection or a longer or different cable.","The insulation resistance test voltage for a 230 V circuit is…":"The insulation resistance test voltage for a 230 V circuit is 500 V DC.","The minimum insulation resistance for a circuit up to 500 V is…":"The minimum insulation resistance for a circuit up to 500 V is 1.0 MΩ.","A reading below… should be investigated even though it is above the minimum.":"A reading below 2 MΩ should be investigated even though it is above the minimum, because it may signal deterioration.","Ten identical circuits of 20 MΩ each, tested together, read about…":"Ten circuits in parallel give 20 ÷ 10 = 2 MΩ.","Before an insulation test the lamps should be…":"Before an insulation test, remove the lamps and close the switches so the whole circuit is tested.","Reversed polarity at a lampholder is dangerous because…":"With reversed polarity the lamp contact remains live when the switch is off.","The centre contact of an Edison screw lampholder is connected to…":"The centre contact of an Edison screw lampholder connects to the line conductor.","Main protective bonding conductors should have a resistance that is…":"Main protective bonding conductors should have a very low resistance, typically below 0.05 Ω.","Parallel paths during a continuity test can…":"Parallel paths during a continuity test can give a false low reading because other metalwork carries part of the test current.","After an insulation test, the circuit is…":"After an insulation test, discharge the circuit because it can store a charge.","If continuity leads are not nulled, the reading will…":"If continuity leads are not nulled, the reading includes the lead resistance and is too high.","Insulation resistance tests must not be made…":"Do not insulation test with lamps and electronic equipment connected, because they may be damaged or give false readings.","A reading of 999 MΩ (above range) on a new circuit indicates…":"A reading above range, such as 999 MΩ, on a new circuit indicates excellent insulation.","The R2 method of continuity testing uses…":"The R2 method uses a long test lead from the main earthing terminal to measure the cpc resistance.","Live tests are carried out…":"Live tests are carried out only after the dead tests are satisfactory.","When measuring Ze, the main earthing conductor is…":"When measuring Ze, disconnect the main earthing conductor from the MET (installation isolated) so only the supply path is measured.","Zs for a circuit is predicted by…":"Zs is predicted by Zs = Ze + (R1 + R2).","With Ze = 0.35 Ω and R1 + R2 = 0.39 Ω, the predicted Zs is…":"Zs = 0.35 + 0.39 = 0.74 Ω.","The maximum Zs for a type B 32 A MCB (0.4 s) is about…":"The maximum Zs for a Type B 32 A MCB (0.4 s) is about 1.37 Ω.","A measured Zs is normally compared with about… of the tabulated maximum.":"Measured Zs is compared with about 80% of the tabulated maximum, to allow for warm conductors.","The prospective earth fault current with Ze = 0.35 Ω on 230 V is about…":"The prospective earth fault current is 230 ÷ 0.35 = 657 A.","At one half of the rated residual current the RCD…":"At half its rated residual current an RCD must not trip.","A general-type 30 mA RCD must trip within… at its rated residual current.":"A general-type 30 mA RCD must trip within 300 ms at its rated residual current.","A 30 mA RCD for additional protection must trip within… at five times its rated current.":"At five times its rated current a 30 mA RCD must trip within 40 ms.","A ramp test on a 30 mA RCD gives a trip current of 24 mA. This is…":"A 30 mA RCD should trip between about half (15 mA) and its full rating (30 mA) on a ramp test. 24 mA is within that range, so it is acceptable.","The RCD test button…":"The test button checks the RCD mechanism. It does not replace an instrument test of the trip time and current.","The earth electrode of a TT system must satisfy…":"For a TT system the electrode resistance must satisfy Ra × IΔn ≤ 50 V.","An electrode resistance of 100 Ω with a 30 mA RCD gives a touch voltage of…":"Touch voltage = 100 Ω × 0.03 A = 3 V.","Functional testing includes…":"Functional testing includes operating switchgear, controls, interlocks and safety devices.","The voltage drop limit for lighting on a 230 V supply is…":"The voltage drop limit for lighting on a 230 V supply is 3%, which is 6.9 V.","Zs should be measured with the instrument on…":"On an RCD-protected circuit, measure Zs on a no-trip (low-current) setting so the RCD does not trip during the test.","In the ring test, r1 and rn should be…":"In the ring test, r1 and rn should be almost equal, because both conductors are the same size and length.","For 2.5 mm² line and neutral with a 1.5 mm² CPC, r2 is about… times r1.":"For 2.5 mm² line and neutral with a 1.5 mm² cpc, r2 is about 1.6 to 1.7 times r1.","In the line-neutral cross-connection, readings at each socket should be about…":"With line and neutral cross-connected, each socket reads about (r1 + rn) ÷ 4, and almost equal at every socket.","In the line-CPC cross-connection, the readings are about…":"With line and cpc cross-connected, readings are about (r1 + r2) ÷ 4.","In the ring test the highest R1 + R2 value from the line-CPC readings is…":"The highest R1 + R2 value from the line-cpc readings is recorded as the circuit's R1 + R2.","A 60 m ring of 2.5 mm² has an end-to-end line resistance of about…":"Resistance = 7.41 mΩ/m × 60 m = 0.44 Ω.","The (r1 + rn) ÷ 4 reading for r1 = rn = 0.445 Ω is about…":"(r1 + rn) ÷ 4 = (0.445 + 0.445) ÷ 4 = 0.22 Ω.","Instruments for installation testing conform to…":"Installation test instruments conform to BS EN 61557 and use GS 38 leads.","Test instruments are normally recalibrated…":"Test instruments are normally recalibrated every year, or as the manufacturer specifies.","Before using a continuity instrument you should…":"Null the leads and check the instrument before use, so lead resistance is not included.","CAT III or CAT IV rated instruments are used at…":"CAT III or CAT IV instruments are used at distribution boards and the origin of the supply, where transient voltages are higher.","Measuring Ze with the main earthing conductor connected would…":"Measuring Ze with the main earthing conductor connected gives a falsely low value because of parallel earth paths.","A loop impedance tester on a high-current setting on an RCD-protected circuit may…":"A loop tester on a high-current setting on an RCD-protected circuit may trip the RCD during the test.","An EICR is a report on…":"An EICR is a report on the condition of an existing installation.","The people who sign an EIC are…":"An EIC is signed by the designer, the constructor and the inspector.","Code C1 on an EICR means…":"Code C1: danger present, immediate remedial action required.","Code C2 on an EICR means…":"Code C2: potentially dangerous, urgent remedial action required.","Code C3 on an EICR means…":"Code C3: improvement recommended.","Code FI on an EICR means…":"Code FI: further investigation required without delay.","An EICR with only C3 observations is…":"An EICR with only C3 observations is satisfactory.","An EICR with a C2 observation is…":"An EICR with a C2 observation is unsatisfactory.","The recommended interval for a privately rented dwelling is…":"The recommended interval for a privately rented dwelling is 5 years.","The recommended interval for an industrial installation is…":"The recommended interval for an industrial installation is 3 years.","The recommended interval for a swimming pool installation is…":"The recommended interval for a swimming pool installation is 1 year.","The recommended interval for a construction site installation is…":"The recommended interval for a construction site installation is 3 months.","The extent and limitations of a periodic inspection are…":"The extent and limitations of a periodic inspection are agreed with the client and recorded on the report.","The schedule of test results records…":"The schedule of test results records the test results for each circuit, including Zs, insulation and RCD times.","In the worked example, a 60 m ring with r1 = 0.47 Ω and r2 = 0.78 Ω has r2 ÷ r1 of about…":"r2 ÷ r1 = 0.78 ÷ 0.47 = 1.66, consistent with the expected figure of about 1.67.","At handover, the client should be shown how to…":"At handover, show the client how to test the RCD with its test button, and give them the certificates.","A certificate should be signed…":"Sign a certificate only after the tests are completed and the results are satisfactory.","PV commissioning (BS EN 62446-1) includes…":"PV commissioning to BS EN 62446-1 includes polarity, open-circuit voltage, short-circuit current and DC insulation tests.","The first step of an electrical design is to…":"The first step of an electrical design is to establish the client's requirements and the loads.","Under CDM 2015 a designer must…":"Under CDM 2015 a designer must consider and reduce health and safety risks through the design.","Which part of BS 7671 requires the assessment of external influences?":"Part 3 of BS 7671 requires assessment of general characteristics, including external influences.","An outdoor luminaire exposed to rain should have a high…":"An outdoor luminaire exposed to rain needs a high IP rating such as IP65.","The design current of a 3 kW, 230 V resistive heater is about…":"Ib = 3,000 ÷ 230 = 13 A.","The input power of a 7.5 kW motor at 88% efficiency is about…":"Input power = 7.5 ÷ 0.88 = 8.5 kW.","The line current of a three-phase motor is calculated using…":"Line current = P ÷ (√3 × V_L × cos φ × efficiency).","An 11 kW motor with efficiency 0.88 and power factor 0.85 on 400 V draws about…":"Input = 11,000 ÷ 0.88 = 12.5 kW. I = 12,500 ÷ (√3 × 400 × 0.85) = 21 A.","Diversity is used because…":"Diversity is used because not all loads operate at the same time.","A cooker diversity allowance is typically…":"A cooker diversity allowance is typically 10 A + 30% of the remainder + 5 A if the control unit has a socket.","An 11 kW cooker at 230 V has a diversified design current of about…":"Full load = 11,000 ÷ 230 = 47.8 A. Diversity = 10 + 0.3 × 37.8 + 5 = 26.3 A.","Maximum demand of loads 3 kW × 0.75, 10 kW × 0.5, 8.5 kW × 1.0 and 6 kW × 0.8 is…":"Maximum demand = 2.25 + 5 + 8.5 + 4.8 = 20.55 kW.","Spare capacity is commonly added to the maximum demand to…":"Spare capacity allows for future expansion.","A workshop with a maximum demand of 20.55 kW at power factor 0.9 on 400 V three-phase draws about…":"I = 20,550 ÷ (√3 × 400 × 0.9) = 33 A per phase.","The design should take the supply Ze from…":"Take Ze from the network operator or by measurement.","A TT system relies on RCDs because…":"On a TT system the earth electrode resistance is high, so the fault current is low and an RCD is needed.","The usual maximum value quoted for Ze on a TN-C-S supply is about…":"The usual maximum Ze quoted for TN-C-S is about 0.35 Ω.","The usual maximum value quoted for Ze on a TN-S supply is about…":"The usual maximum Ze quoted for TN-S is about 0.8 Ω.","Main bonding for a TN-C-S supply with a supply neutral up to 35 mm² is typically at least…":"Main bonding for TN-C-S with a supply neutral up to 35 mm² is typically at least 10 mm².","The minimum size of copper main protective bonding for a TN-S installation is…":"The minimum size of copper main protective bonding for TN-S is 6 mm².","Dividing lighting over more than one circuit means…":"Dividing lighting over more than one circuit means one fault does not leave a whole area dark.","Using several RCDs or RCBOs ensures…":"Using several RCDs or RCBOs ensures a single earth fault does not shut down the whole installation.","Balancing single-phase loads across the three phases…":"Balancing single-phase loads across the three phases reduces neutral current and uneven loading.","A distribution board should normally be located…":"A distribution board should be close to the centre of the load, to keep cables short.","Spare ways are provided in a distribution board for…":"Spare ways are provided in a distribution board for future circuits.","Appendix 5 of BS 7671 classifies…":"Appendix 5 of BS 7671 classifies external influences such as water, solid objects and temperature.","The overload protection condition is…":"The overload protection condition is Ib ≤ In ≤ Iz.","For an MCB the conventional operating current I2 is…":"For an MCB the conventional operating current I2 is 1.45 × In.","The breaking capacity of a device must be at least…":"The breaking capacity of a device must be at least the prospective fault current at its location.","A type C MCB trips magnetically at…":"A Type C MCB trips magnetically at 5 to 10 times In.","Which MCB type suits a transformer with high inrush current?":"Type D suits a transformer, because it tolerates a high inrush current.","Which type of RCD detects smooth DC residual currents?":"Type B RCDs detect smooth DC residual currents as well as AC.","Let-through energy I²t is…":"Let-through energy I²t is the energy the device lets through during a fault.","The maximum Zs for a type B 32 A MCB (0.4 s) is…":"The maximum Zs for a Type B 32 A MCB (0.4 s) is 1.37 Ω.","The maximum Zs for a type C 32 A MCB (0.4 s) is…":"The maximum Zs for a Type C 32 A MCB (0.4 s) is 0.68 Ω, lower because it trips at a higher current.","Discrimination between two fuses is achieved if the upstream-to-downstream rating ratio is at least about…":"Fuse discrimination is achieved if the upstream-to-downstream rating ratio is at least about 1.6 : 1.","RCD discrimination is achieved by using…":"RCD discrimination uses a time-delayed (Type S) RCD upstream of instantaneous RCDs.","An SPD protects against…":"An SPD protects against transient overvoltages.","BS 3036 rewireable fuses operate at about…":"BS 3036 rewireable fuses operate at about 1.9 times their rated current, so the cable must be derated.","The correction factor for a BS 3036 fuse is…":"The correction factor for a BS 3036 fuse is 0.725.","Back-up (cascading) protection means…":"Back-up (cascading) protection means an upstream device limits the fault current so a downstream device of lower breaking capacity is not overloaded.","AFDDs are used to…":"AFDDs detect dangerous arc faults.","The minimum tabulated cable rating is calculated as…":"The minimum tabulated cable rating is It ≥ In ÷ (Ca × Cg × Ci × Cc).","A 20 A circuit, Ca = 0.94 and Cg = 0.80 needs a minimum tabulated rating of about…":"It = 20 ÷ (0.94 × 0.80) = 26.6 A.","With In = 32 A, Ca = 0.94 and Cg = 0.80 the minimum tabulated rating is about…":"It = 32 ÷ (0.94 × 0.80) = 42.6 A.","Voltage drop is calculated as…":"Voltage drop = (mV/A/m × Ib × L) ÷ 1,000.","The voltage drop limit for a lighting circuit on 230 V is…":"The limit for lighting on 230 V is 3%, which is 6.9 V.","The voltage drop limit for other circuits on 230 V is…":"The limit for other circuits on 230 V is 5%, which is 11.5 V.","The voltage drop for 29 mV/A/m, 5 A and 40 m is…":"Voltage drop = 29 × 5 × 40 ÷ 1,000 = 5.8 V.","A 25 kW load at 400 V, pf 0.9, three-phase draws about…":"I = 25,000 ÷ (√3 × 400 × 0.9) = 40 A.","The voltage drop for 3.8 mV/A/m, 40.1 A over 35 m is about…":"Voltage drop = 3.8 × 40.1 × 35 ÷ 1,000 = 5.3 V.","The three-phase mV/A/m values are about… of the single-phase values.":"Three-phase mV/A/m values are about 0.87 of the single-phase values.","A 9.5 kW shower on 230 V has a design current of about…":"Ib = 9,500 ÷ 230 = 41.3 A.","Ci for a cable surrounded by thermal insulation is…":"Ci for a cable surrounded by thermal insulation is 0.5.","Cg for three circuits grouped together is typically…":"Cg for three grouped circuits is typically 0.70.","Ca at 35 degrees Celsius for a 70 degree PVC cable is…":"Ca at 35°C for 70°C PVC cable is 0.94.","If voltage drop fails you should…":"If voltage drop fails, use a larger cable, shorten the run or reduce the load.","Which reference method is a cable clipped direct to a wall?":"Reference Method C is a cable clipped direct to a wall.","A cable run of 20 m with 7.3 mV/A/m carrying 41.3 A has a voltage drop of about…":"Voltage drop = 7.3 × 41.3 × 20 ÷ 1,000 = 6.0 V.","Which must be checked after the current rating of a cable has been selected?":"After the current rating is selected, check voltage drop and Zs.","An 8 kW single-phase resistive load at 230 V has a design current of about…":"Ib = 8,000 ÷ 230 = 34.8 A.","A cable with a tabulated rating of 62 A, Ca = 0.94 and Cg = 0.80 has an installed capacity of about…":"Installed capacity = 62 × 0.94 × 0.80 = 46.6 A.","The prospective earth fault current with Zs = 0.74 Ω on 230 V is about…":"Fault current = 230 ÷ 0.74 = 311 A.","A fault with a source impedance of 0.05 Ω and cable impedance of 0.10 Ω gives a fault current of…":"Fault current = 230 ÷ (0.05 + 0.10) = 1,533 A.","The adiabatic equation is…":"The adiabatic equation is S = √(I² × t) ÷ k.","The value of k for a copper conductor with 70 degree PVC insulation is…":"k = 115 for copper with 70°C PVC insulation.","The value of k for copper with XLPE or EPR insulation is…":"k = 143 for copper with XLPE or EPR insulation.","A 1,200 A fault cleared in 0.01 s needs a minimum copper PVC conductor of about…":"S = √(1,200² × 0.01) ÷ 115 = 120 ÷ 115 = 1.04 mm².","The same 1,200 A fault cleared in 0.4 s needs a minimum conductor of about…":"S = √(1,200² × 0.4) ÷ 115 = 759 ÷ 115 = 6.6 mm².","With a let-through energy of 80,000 A²s the minimum copper PVC conductor is about…":"S = √80,000 ÷ 115 = 283 ÷ 115 = 2.5 mm².","For a line conductor of 16 mm² or less, the CPC (Table 54.7) is…":"For a line conductor of 16 mm² or less, the cpc is the same size.","For a line conductor over 35 mm², the CPC is…":"For a line conductor over 35 mm², the cpc is half the size of the line conductor.","The main bonding conductor on a non-PME supply is at least…":"The main bonding conductor on a non-PME supply is at least 6 mm².","A quick-acting protective device allows…":"A quick-acting protective device produces less heat, so a smaller cpc is allowed.","The lumen method formula is…":"The lumen method formula is N = (E × A) ÷ (F × UF × MF).","A classroom 8 m × 6 m, 300 lux, 4,000 lm luminaires, UF 0.55, MF 0.8 needs about…":"N = (300 × 48) ÷ (4,000 × 0.55 × 0.8) = 14,400 ÷ 1,760 = 8.2, so fit 9.","The maintenance factor allows for…":"The maintenance factor allows for dirt and lamp ageing.","The room index is calculated as…":"Room index = (L × W) ÷ (Hm × (L + W)).","Escape routes in emergency lighting need at least…":"Escape routes need at least 1 lux along the centre line.","Emergency lighting normally has a duration of at least…":"Emergency lighting normally has a duration of at least 3 hours.","The capacitor needed to correct 100 kW from pf 0.75 to 0.95 is about…":"Capacitor = 100 × (tan φ₁ − tan φ₂) = 100 × (0.882 − 0.329) = 55 kVAr.","Over-correcting power factor can cause…":"Over-correcting gives a leading power factor and voltage problems.","A circuit schedule includes…":"A circuit schedule includes Ib, In, cable size, length, voltage drop and Zs for each circuit.","A type D 16 A MCB is chosen for a motor with starting current 101 A because…":"A Type D 16 A MCB trips between 160 and 320 A, above the 101 A start, so it does not trip on starting.","In the motor example the overload relay is set to…":"The overload relay is set to the full-load current of 14.5 A.","The Joint Industry Board (JIB) is involved with…":"The JIB deals with employment terms, grading and the registration of electricians.","NICEIC, NAPIT and Certsure are…":"NICEIC, NAPIT and Certsure are competent person scheme organisations.","The ECA represents…":"The ECA represents electrical contractors.","An example of a growth area in electrotechnical work is…":"EV charging and renewable energy installation are growth areas in electrotechnical work.","An inspector and tester mainly…":"An inspector and tester mainly carries out inspection, testing and certification of installations.","The AM2 or AM2E is…":"The AM2 or AM2E is a practical end-point competence assessment for electricians.","The qualification that covers inspection and testing of electrical installations is…":"City & Guilds 2391 is the qualification that covers inspection and testing.","CPD stands for…":"CPD stands for continuing professional development.","EngTech, IEng and CEng are…":"EngTech, IEng and CEng are levels of professional registration through the IET and other institutions.","An ECS card shows that…":"An ECS card shows that the holder has the relevant electrotechnical qualification and safety assessment.","An apprenticeship combines…":"An apprenticeship combines paid work, study with a training provider and an end-point assessment.","Keeping up to date with new editions of BS 7671 is part of…":"Keeping up to date with new editions of BS 7671 is part of CPD.","After the Level 2 diploma, the usual next step towards approved electrician status is…":"After the Level 2 diploma, the usual next step is the Level 3 qualification, experience and the AM2.","Under the Working Time Regulations the standard maximum average week is…":"Under the Working Time Regulations the standard maximum average week is 48 hours, unless you opt out.","A worker is entitled to a break of 20 minutes if the working day is longer than…":"A worker is entitled to a 20-minute break if the working day is longer than six hours.","Daily rest between working days is normally at least…":"Daily rest between working days is normally at least 11 hours.","Paid annual leave is at least…":"Paid annual leave is at least 5.6 weeks, which is 28 days for a full-time five-day week.","The Equality Act 2010 protects people from…":"The Equality Act 2010 protects people from discrimination because of protected characteristics such as age, disability, race and sex.","PPE required for work should be…":"PPE required for work should be provided free by the employer.","A worker who raises a genuine safety concern…":"A worker who raises a genuine safety concern is protected from being punished for doing so.","Whistleblowing protection applies to…":"Whistleblowing protection applies to workers who report wrongdoing in the public interest.","A self-employed electrician must register with…":"A self-employed electrician must register with HMRC for tax and National Insurance.","CIS stands for…":"CIS stands for Construction Industry Scheme.","Insurance that a self-employed electrician needs includes…":"A self-employed electrician needs public liability insurance, among others.","To certify notifiable work in dwellings, a self-employed electrician should be registered with…":"To certify notifiable work in dwellings, register with a competent person scheme.","Professional conduct includes…":"Professional conduct includes working within your competence and not compromising safety.","Good business practice includes…":"Good business practice includes clear written quotations, agreed payment terms and keeping records.","Which would support a job application?":"A CV listing qualifications, experience and certificates supports a job application.","The responsibility for the health and safety of themselves and others on a job lies with…":"Responsibility for health and safety on a job lies with the self-employed electrician as well as the client and others.","A facilities management company mainly…":"A facilities management company mainly maintains buildings and their services, such as electrical systems.","Infrastructure work for electricians includes…":"Infrastructure work for electricians includes rail, road and energy network electrical systems.","A data centre needs electricians because it relies on…":"A data centre needs electricians because it relies on highly reliable power distribution, standby supplies and cooling.","An estimator in an electrical firm…":"An estimator in an electrical firm prepares costs and quotations for work.","Which assessment is a practical test of competence for electricians?":"The AM2 or AM2E is a practical test of competence for electricians.","A portfolio of evidence for an NVQ records…":"A portfolio of evidence for an NVQ records the work experience and tasks the learner has carried out.","The IET offers…":"The IET offers professional registration such as EngTech, IEng and CEng.","Level 3 competence assessment is mainly carried out…":"Level 3 competence assessment is mainly carried out in the workplace or at an assessment centre by observation of practical work.","The apprentice rate of the minimum wage applies to…":"The apprentice rate of the minimum wage applies to apprentices under 19 or in the first year of their apprenticeship.","A worker on a zero-hours contract…":"A worker on a zero-hours contract still has employment rights such as the minimum wage and paid holiday."};
if(!window.Z||!Z.mods)return;const seen=new Set();const fix=q=>{if(Array.isArray(q)&&E[q[0]]&&!seen.has(q)){seen.add(q);q[3]=E[q[0]]}};
[window.Y,window.Z,window.X].forEach(s=>{if(!s)return;(s.mods||[]).forEach(m=>(m.q||[]).forEach(fix));Object.keys(s).forEach(k=>{const v=s[k];if(v&&Array.isArray(v.q))v.q.forEach(fix)})});})();
